<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schleswig-Holstein</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Schleswig-Holstein is the northernmost state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>. It takes its name from the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein, ruled by the Danish king for centuries, whose status provoked the Schleswig Wars of 1848–1851 and 1864. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed both duchies in 1867, and northern Schleswig returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> after a plebiscite in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein.jpg" alt="Schleswig-Holstein" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Harald Søby · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Schleswig-Holstein.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Schleswig-Holstein lies at the base of the Jutland Peninsula between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, and covers 15,763 km2. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The state consists of Holstein, the southern part of the former Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, Lauenburg and the formerly independent city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>. The Kiel Canal connects Brunsbüttel on the North Sea with Kiel on the Baltic Sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Saxon Holstein became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s Saxon campaigns in the late 8th century, and from 811 the River Eider was its northern border. Between 500 and 1200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. In the 12th century Duke Abel came into conflict with his brother King Eric IV and won autonomy for Schleswig as a duchy. From 1460 the Danish king ruled both Schleswig and Holstein as their duke, although Holstein belonged to the Holy Roman Empire.</p><p>In the 19th century both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and Germans claimed the duchies, in a dispute called the Schleswig-Holstein Question. Germans wanted both to join the German Confederation and cited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ribe">Treaty of Ribe</a>, by which they were to stay forever undivided, while Danes held that the Eider was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s natural border. Holstein was entirely German-speaking, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> was predominantly Danish-speaking until a shift to German began in the south in the late 18th and early 19th centuries.</p><p>In 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> tried to annex the area and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> invaded, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-schleswig-war">First Schleswig War</a>, which Denmark won. Prussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> of 1864, and the territory became the Prussian Province of Schleswig-Holstein rather than the independent state that German Schleswig-Holsteiners had hoped for.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> arranged plebiscites in 1920, and the Danish-speaking north returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the province came under British occupation, and on 23 August 1946 the military government reconstituted it as a separate state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Schleswig-Holstein is the northernmost of the sixteen German states, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> and other main cities at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flensburg">Flensburg</a>. German is its official language, and Low German, Danish and North Frisian are recognised minority languages. Its culture combines Danish, Frisian and German elements.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schleswig-Holstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government raised a <b>Schleswig-Holstein</b> army and received support from Prussia and the German Confederation, whose troops fought the Danes in 1848-1849.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-schleswig-holstein">Provisional Government of Schleswig-Holstein</a></li></ul></section>
